\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Bangla NLP resources, dialect resources and the gap addressed by this thesis.}}{2}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Overview of the proposed route, normalize, then ground framework.}}{6}{figure.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Inference pipeline. A question passes the safety rules, is routed by language variety, normalized if it is dialectal or romanized, routed by domain, checked by the controller, grounded in three passages from its domain's knowledge base, answered by that domain's LoRA expert and finally checked by the output guard. The two dark boxes are LoRA adapters on one shared 4-bit Gemma-4-E4B-it model and are the only steps that run the language model; everything else runs on a CPU.\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces LoRA: the pretrained weight stays frozen; only the low-rank matrices $A$ and $B$ are trained. Swapping $(A,B)$ swaps the skill while the base model stays in memory once.\relax }}{34}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Weight memory per numeric format for $4\times 10^9$ parameters (bytes per parameter: FP32 4, BF16 2, INT8 1, NF4 with double quantization $\approx $4.127 bits \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {dettmers2023qlora}). Activations, the LoRA weights and the optimizer come on top.\relax }}{34}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Retrieval-augmented generation: the expert answers from retrieved evidence, or writes a fixed ``not found'' sentence when the evidence does not contain the answer.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Token-level sparse MoE vs.\ the system-level Mixture-of-Adapters used here: one adapter for rewriting, then the adapter of the predicted domain for answering.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Development workflow. Teal stages run on a CPU, dark stages on one rented A40 GPU; dashed stages are the optional DEV-selected improvements (Section~\ref {sec:v2}). About 40 GPU-hours (US\$20) were used up to S6.\relax }}{36}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces The three data roles and the audit that keeps them apart. Test passages of NCTB-QA and BanglaRQA are in the KB (open-book QA needs them), but test questions and medical test answers never are.\relax }}{37}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Unique dialect--Standard pairs per source after cleaning and cross-source de-duplication (38,283 in total; 16,905 Chittagonian and 13,855 Sylheti in Bangla script, 7,523 romanized).\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Left: character 3-gram Jaccard between each dialect sentence and its Standard translation (a peak near 0 means almost every trigram changed), and question length per domain. Right: the balanced domain-router data.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces How much of the Standard sentence survives in the dialect sentence (Bangla-script pairs, all splits).\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Cleaning log. Duplicates dominate: ChatgaiyyaBench re-distributes Vashantor and ChatgaiyyaAlap sentences. Doctor QA shrinks from 5,135 to 4,417 pairs.\relax }}{39}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Target mix of the QA experts' training data: a third of the targets teach the expert to say ``not found'' instead of guessing (RAFT-style training \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {zhang2024raft}).\relax }}{40}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Tokenizer fertility on Standard, Chittagonian and Sylheti text. Dialect text costs 30--40\% more tokens than Standard text for the Gemma tokenizers.\relax }}{41}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.14}{\ignorespaces Deterministic safety: emergency rules before and after normalization, and the output guard on every medical answer.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.15}{\ignorespaces The controller's 20 features and its three decisions.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces Building the paired test set. Because the dialect versions are model-written and ``lighter'' than real dialect text, a second evaluation uses 481 questions written by native speakers (Section~\ref {sec:human}).\relax }}{46}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Retrieval in system v2 as designed. After the DEV decisions (Table~\ref {tab:v2}), the adopted path is: normalized question $\rightarrow $ 50 candidates from the domain's KB, plus 50 from the other KB when the domain router's confidence is below 0.9 (I5) $\rightarrow $ near-duplicate removal (education) $\rightarrow $ cross-encoder re-ranking $\rightarrow $ top 3. The glossary (I8) and RRF fusion (I9) were tested but not adopted.\relax }}{48}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.18}{\ignorespaces GPU time per stage: about 40 A40 GPU-hours ($\approx $US\$20 at US\$0.49/h) up to S6; system v2 added about 2 more hours.\relax }}{49}{figure.caption.42}%
